# Ctrl-Z on a *foreground* block containing a `cmd` (`{ { cmd sleep N } }`), then `bg`,
# then killing the process with `host:signal`, must leave the `cmd` job reported as
# `Terminated` -- same as `pty_ctrl_z_bg_then_signal_shows_terminated.crush`, but nested.
#
# This shape pauses only the innermost statement's job (the one `current_job()` finds),
# and no `Job::eval` ever registers it as a background job, so "this job left the
# foreground" can't be tied to that registration -- it's recorded by
# `GlobalState::pause` itself (see `JobData::backgrounded`). Found while enumerating
# every way a job can stop holding the foreground.
#
# The paused job's id is looked up (highest-id `Paused` row) rather than hardcoded, since
# it depends on how many intermediate block jobs exist. `pty_child_pid` finds the real
# external `sleep`'s pid once, before Ctrl-Z -- `bg` only sends SIGCONT to the same
# process (no new fork), so that pid is still valid after Ctrl-Z/`bg` and is reused
# directly for `host:signal`, rather than re-deriving it a second time.
#
# KNOWN GAP: sending Ctrl-Z itself is NOT converted to the counted-prompt/
# `pty_send_control` approach the other 7 Ctrl-Z/Ctrl-C pty tests now use (see
# pty_test_helpers.crush): tried directly, converted this exact way, and found it
# genuinely unreliable here specifically -- roughly 2 in 3 runs failed with no job ever
# reaching `Paused` at all, even though the *simpler*, non-nested Ctrl-Z cases (a bare
# `sleep`, a bare `cmd sleep`) convert cleanly and have run clean well over 100 times
# each, including under artificial CPU load, with the exact same technique. Three
# levels of nested block/closure dispatch before the innermost `cmd` job exists appears
# to measurably widen the real window `pty_ctrl_z_suspends_external_job.crush`'s own
# header already documents (Ctrl-Z racing the fork/exec/terminal-handoff) -- confirmed
# the race is real and not just an artifact of the test by adding targeted tracing,
# which consistently made it stop reproducing entirely (added latency on the
# signal-handling thread gives the nested dispatch enough extra time to finish first) --
# the exact signature of a genuine, very narrow timing race, not a logic bug in this
# file's own retry helper. The fixed delay right after Ctrl-Z is left in place pending
# that investigation, same as `pty_fg_then_signal_leaves_no_terminated_row.crush`'s own
# documented gap.
#
# What *is* resolved without guessing: once Ctrl-Z has landed, `GlobalState::pause`
# sends its target job's controller a `Pause` message over a channel -- a send that
# returns immediately, well before `cmd.rs`'s worker thread actually gets around to
# delivering the real `SIGSTOP` to the external process (see `wait_for_child_inner`'s
# `Pause` arm in control/cmd.rs). So the external process's own OS-level state
# (exposed by `host:procs`'s `status` column, backed by `sysinfo::Process::status`)
# only ever flips to "Stopped" *after* `GlobalState::pause`'s synchronous status flip
# has already happened -- waiting for it, directly from out here with no round trip
# through the nested session's own `jobs` table at all, is a strictly later but
# sufficient, OS-observable proxy for "this pause has genuinely landed," confirmed
# directly: `kill -STOP`/`kill -CONT` on a plain background process flip `host:procs`'s
# `status` between "Runnable" and "Stopped" exactly as expected.
source tests/pty_test_helpers.crush
$t := $(pty_spawn)
$n := $(pty_enable_counted_prompt $t)

$t:write "{ { cmd sleep 7432 } }\n"
$t:wait_for "sleep"
$pid := $(pty_child_pid $t ^(.*sleep))
$t:write $pty:control:z
$t:wait_for "# "
sleep 500ms

$target_pid := $pid
$os_status := ""
$tries := 0
while {(($tries < 20) and ($os_status != "Stopped"))} {
    $os_status = ($(host:procs | where {($pid == $target_pid)} | select status | list:collect status))[0]
    if (($os_status) != "Stopped") {
        sleep 50ms
        $tries = ($tries + 1)
    }
}
assert (($os_status) == "Stopped") $("expected pid {} to reach OS status Stopped within the retry budget, found {}":format $target_pid $os_status)

$n = $(pty_run $t $n "$bgid := ($(jobs | where {($status == \"Paused\")} | select id | sort id reverse=$true))[0]:id")
$n = $(pty_run $t $n "bg $bgid")

host:signal $pid signal=SIGKILL
$n = $(pty_run $t $n "$n := $(jobs | where {($status == \"Terminated\")} | count)")
$t:write "echo $(\"NESTED_Z_CHECK_{}\":format $n)\n"
$t:wait_for "NESTED_Z_CHECK_1"

$t:close
